.\" Copyright (c) 2026 NFS-Ganesha / ntirpc contributors
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt RPC_SVC_ZEROCOPY 3
.Os ntirpc
.Sh NAME
.Nm rpc_svc_zerocopy ,
.Nm svc_init ,
.Nm svc_set_socket_zerocopy ,
.Nm svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats ,
.Nm svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats
.Nd TCP socket transmit zerocopy for RPC replies
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libntirpc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In rpc/rpc.h
.Ft bool
.Fn svc_init "svc_init_params *params"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy "bool enable"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats "struct svc_socket_zerocopy_stats *out"
.Ft void
.Fn svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On Linux, ntirpc can send large connection-oriented (TCP) RPC replies with
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
after
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
is set on the accepted socket.
This avoids copying userspace payload into kernel skbuffs for eligible sends.
Buffers remain pinned until the kernel posts a completion on the socket
.Em error queue
.Pq Dv MSG_ERRQUEUE ,
signaled as
.Dv EPOLLERR
/
.Dv POLLERR
(not ordinary
.Dv EPOLLIN
/
.Dv EPOLLOUT ) .
ntirpc drains completions, then destroys the reply XDR / gifted
.Pq Dv UIO_FLAG_REFER
buffers.
.Pp
The feature is optional, off by default, and requires kernel/glibc support
for
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
and
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
.Pq CMake
.Dv HAVE_SO_ZEROCOPY .
If
.Fn setsockopt
fails, the transport falls back to classic
.Fn sendmsg .
.Ss Enabling via svc_init
Pass fields in
.Vt svc_init_params
before
.Fn svc_init :
.Bl -tag -width socket_zerocopy_max_pending
.It Va socket_zerocopy
When
.Dv true ,
enable
.Dv SO_ZEROCOPY
on newly accepted TCP sockets and allow
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
for large sends.
Default:
.Dv false .
.It Va socket_zerocopy_threshold
Minimum send size in bytes to attempt
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY .
Zero selects the ntirpc default (16 KiB).
Ordinary (non-refer) payloads use twice this threshold.
.It Va socket_zerocopy_max_pending
Cap on outstanding uncompleted zerocopy payload bytes per transport.
Zero selects the ntirpc default (64 MiB).
Sends that would exceed the cap use classic
.Fn sendmsg .
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
svc_init_params p = { 0 };
p.flags = SVC_INIT_EPOLL;
p.socket_zerocopy = true;
p.socket_zerocopy_threshold = 16384;      /* or 0 for default */
p.socket_zerocopy_max_pending = 0;        /* default 64MiB */
if (!svc_init(&p))
	err(1, "svc_init");
.Ed
.Ss Runtime toggle
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy
enables or disables the feature after init.
It affects subsequent eligibility checks and new accepts that call
.Fn svc_zc_try_enable ;
sockets that already have
.Dv SVC_XPRT_FLAG_SO_ZEROCOPY
set keep the socket option until closed.
.Ss Behavior notes
.Bl -bullet
.It
Only TCP service transports
.Pq see Fn svc_vc_create
participate; datagram and raw transports do not.
.It
.Dv ENOBUFS
/
.Dv EOPNOTSUPP
on a zerocopy send fall back to a classic send for that chunk.
.It
Loopback traffic typically completes with
.Dv SO_EE_CODE_ZEROCOPY_COPIED
(deferred copy); the API and buffer lifetime rules are the same.
After the first such completion on a transport, ntirpc clears
.Dv SVC_XPRT_FLAG_SO_ZEROCOPY
so subsequent sends on that socket omit
.Dv MSG_ZEROCOPY
(kernel guidance: the flag is a sticky signal for the socket, not a transient
glitch). In-flight completions are still drained.
.It
Applications that gift buffers into the XDR ioq
.Pq e.g. Ceph FSAL zerocopy READ
must not free them until ntirpc destroys the ioq after ERRQUEUE completion.
.It
This is independent of any application-level zerocopy into ntirpc
.Pq userspace buffer handoff .
Both layers can be used together for end-to-end savings.
.El
.Ss NFS-Ganesha configuration
When used under NFS-Ganesha, the same knobs are exposed as
.Va NFS_Core_Param
options (mapped into
.Vt svc_init_params ) :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
NFS_Core_Param {
	Enable_Socket_Zerocopy = true;
	Socket_Zerocopy_Threshold = 16384;   /* 0 = ntirpc default */
	Socket_Zerocopy_Max_Pending = 0;     /* 0 = ntirpc default */
}
.Ed
.Pp
See also Ganesha documentation
.Pa src/doc/socket_zerocopy.md
in the NFS-Ganesha tree.
.Ss Statistics
.Fn svc_get_socket_zerocopy_stats
returns a snapshot of
.Va sends ,
.Va completions ,
.Va copied
.Pq Dv SO_EE_CODE_ZEROCOPY_COPIED ,
.Va enobufs ,
and
.Va fallback ,
plus the effective threshold and max-pending.
.Fn svc_reset_socket_zerocopy_stats
clears the counters.
Under NFS-Ganesha these are exposed as DBus method
.Nm GetSocketZerocopyStats
and
.Nm ganesha_stats.py Cm socket_zc
.Pq also Cm json Cm socket_zc ;
.Nm ganesha_stats.py Cm reset
clears them with the other counters.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn svc_init
returns
.Dv true
on success,
.Dv false
on failure.
.Fn svc_set_socket_zerocopy
returns no value.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sendmsg 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr rpc 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_calls 3 ,
.Xr rpc_svc_create 3
.Pp
Linux kernel documentation:
.Lk https://docs.kernel.org/networking/msg_zerocopy.html MSG_ZEROCOPY
.Sh HISTORY
Socket TX zerocopy support was added to ntirpc for large TCP reply sends
with deferred buffer release on ERRQUEUE completion.
